 *   **Accuracy:** Provides visually accurate results by correctly modeling self-attenuation, even when Gaussians do not overlap [S1]. It avoids the "popping" and visibility artifacts associated with approximations when $g(\mathbf{p}) > 1$ [S1].
 *   **Stability:** Removes the need for clamping $f_i(\mathbf{p})$ to $[0,1)$, which was necessary in standard EWA/3DGS to prevent invalid attenuation values [S1]. This ensures gradients remain essential for training and avoids sharp, non-Gaussian falloffs [S1].
-*   **Complexity:** The forward pass complexity is similar to standard splatting, $O(N)$ per pixel, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. The backward pass complexity is also $O(N)$ but requires a front-to-back traversal, which may impact cache efficiency compared to the standard back-to-front approach [S1].
-*   **Performance:** The exact exponential calculation ($e^{-f}$) is slightly more expensive than the Taylor approximation ($1-f$), but the difference is negligible on modern hardware [S1]. The need for a front-to-back backward pass may require additional memory to store intermediate transmittance values or re-computation, but this is mitigated by the fact that the forward pass can also be front-to-back [S1].
-*   **When to use:** Use when high visual fidelity is required, especially for large primitives or scenes where Gaussians have high mass/opacity, causing $f(\mathbf{p})$ to exceed 1. It is particularly important for training stability in 3DGS when using unrestricted opacity parameters [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** If computational resources are extremely limited and the visual difference between the Taylor approximation and the exact exponential is negligible for the specific scene (e.g., very small, low-opacity Gaussians) [S1].
